kwalletd6: ask the service for its collections instead of trusting the cache

Creating a wallet through kwalletd6 can end with the user being asked to
create a second one called "kdewallet__0_" right after "kdewallet".

openInternal() decides whether to create a wallet by looking at
listCollections(). That calls secret_service_load_collections_sync(), which
sounds like it talks to the service but really just re-reads the
"Collections" property cached in libsecret's D-Bus proxy. The cache is only
updated when the service's PropertiesChanged signal is dispatched by our own
main loop, and we don't get back to the main loop in time:

  - Straight after createCollection() we look the new collection up to
    unlock it. It isn't in the cache yet, so the open() that just created
    the wallet returns -1.
  - If another open() was queued while the create dialog was up, it can be
    handled before the pending signal is. It doesn't see the wallet either
    and calls createCollection() again. ksecretd then picks a free name for
    the same label, and the user gets a prompt for "kdewallet__0_".

This is easy to hit when the first wallet is created on demand, e.g. KMail's
account setup on Termux where nothing has created kdewallet beforehand. It
would also explain why creating the wallet in System Settings beforehand
only seems to help after logging out and in again.

Fetch the property from the service ourselves, put it in the proxy's cache,
and only then let libsecret reload. Both callers (listCollections and
retrieveCollection) go through the new helper.

Reproduced outside of KDE with libsecret and a small mock service doing what
ksecretd does on CreateCollection: without this change two back to back
opens create "kdewallet" and "kdewallet__0_" and both fail, with it there is
one wallet and both succeed.

--- a/src/runtime/kwalletd/secretserviceclient.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/kwalletd/secretserviceclient.cpp
@@ -81,6 +81,43 @@
     return false;
 }
 
+// secret_service_load_collections_sync() doesn't actually ask the service for
+// anything: it rebuilds the list from the "Collections" property the D-Bus
+// proxy has cached. That cache only changes once a signal from the service has
+// been dispatched by our main loop, and that hasn't happened yet when we look
+// right after creating a collection, or when the next open() was already
+// sitting in the queue while the create dialog was on screen. The wallet we
+// just made then looks like it isn't there, the open that created it fails,
+// and the next one asks the service to create it again, which is how people
+// end up being asked to create "kdewallet__0_".
+// So fetch the property ourselves first and then let libsecret reload from it.
+static bool reloadCollections(SecretService *service, GError **error)
+{
+    GDBusProxy *proxy = G_DBUS_PROXY(service);
+    GVariant *reply = g_dbus_connection_call_sync(g_dbus_proxy_get_connection(proxy),
+                                                  g_dbus_proxy_get_name(proxy),
+                                                  g_dbus_proxy_get_object_path(proxy),
+                                                  "org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties",
+                                                  "Get",
+                                                  g_variant_new("(ss)", "org.freedesktop.Secret.Service", "Collections"),
+                                                  G_VARIANT_TYPE("(v)"),
+                                                  G_DBUS_CALL_FLAGS_NONE,
+                                                  -1,
+                                                  nullptr,
+                                                  error);
+    if (!reply) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    GVariant *paths = nullptr;
+    g_variant_get(reply, "(v)", &paths);
+    g_dbus_proxy_set_cached_property(proxy, "Collections", paths);
+    g_variant_unref(paths);
+    g_variant_unref(reply);
+
+    return secret_service_load_collections_sync(service, nullptr, error);
+}
+
 static QString typeToString(SecretServiceClient::Type type)
 {
